WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:10.540
Effectively integrating new education initiatives
into classrooms is a big challenge.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.120
Social and emotional learning, or SEL, is
no exception.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.480
Research can help, but only when used wisely.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.320
For one thing, researchers favor work
that tends to tell us "what works".

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.119
The risk is that such research yields “evidence-based
recommendations” that can give short shrift

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:33.300
to what it takes to make a measure work or
how well a program successful in one place

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.730
will translate to another.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:39.840
Another issue: Some of what we most value
in SEL, like students’ sense of belonging

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.400
or their relationship skills, is difficult to measure reliably.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.229
Climate surveys are a start, but they limit
results to a subjective, vague sense of a

00:00:48.229 --> 00:00:51.020
school’s progress in improving student wellbeing.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.730
When the evidence is shaky or the research
uncertain, SEL advocates need to face that

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:56.730
fact head-on.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.920
The solution?

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.530
Transparency, willingness to draw feedback
from supporters and skeptics, and research

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.240
that’s attentive to real-world challenges.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.320
How do you think school's should measure social and emotional learning?

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.180
Let us know in the comments. Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:23.960
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

